# Citation metadata for SpliceCraft.
#
# GitHub reads this file to render the "Cite this repository" button on the
# repository page (APA + BibTeX export). Zenodo reads `.zenodo.json` instead
# whenever both files are present, so the two MUST stay in step: release.py
# stamps `version:` + `date-released:` here and `"version"` there on every
# release, and tests/test_citation.py fails the suite if they ever drift from
# `splicecraft.__version__`. See docs/citation.md for the full story.
#
# The DOI is minted by Zenodo when a GitHub Release is published, so there is
# nothing to record until the first release archives -- this file deliberately
# carries no `identifiers:` block yet. The release that mints it adds one for
# the CONCEPT DOI (the identifier that always resolves to the newest archived
# version); per-version DOIs stay listed on the Zenodo record itself.
# docs/citation.md has the exact post-archive checklist.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: 'SpliceCraft: a terminal-native plasmid workbench for molecular cloning'
message: >-
  If you use SpliceCraft in work you publish, please cite it using the metadata
  below.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Cocioba
    given-names: Sebastian
    affiliation: Binomica Labs
    orcid: https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6821-2996
repository-code: https://github.com/Binomica-Labs/SpliceCraft
url: https://splicecraft.bio
repository-artifact: https://pypi.org/project/splicecraft/
license: MIT
version: 1.2.53
date-released: '2026-08-15'
abstract: >-
  SpliceCraft is a terminal-native plasmid workbench for molecular cloning. It
  renders circular and linear plasmid maps as Unicode braille graphics in any
  modern terminal, edits sequences base by base, designs PCR and mutagenesis
  primers, plans and simulates Golden Braid, MoClo, Gibson, and traditional
  restriction assemblies, runs BLASTN/BLASTP/HMMscan in-process via pyhmmer,
  optimises codon usage, models agarose gels, ingests Sanger and nanopore
  sequencing runs, and keeps a lab notebook -- entirely offline, with no cloud
  account and no data leaving the machine unless the user asks. Written in pure
  Python on Textual and Biopython, it also exposes a local HTTP API and a
  stdlib CLI so external agents and shell scripts can drive every workflow.
keywords:
  - bioinformatics
  - molecular biology
  - synthetic biology
  - plasmid
  - molecular cloning
  - GenBank
  - primer design
  - restriction enzymes
  - Golden Gate
  - Golden Braid
  - MoClo
  - Gibson assembly
  - codon optimization
  - sequence analysis
  - terminal user interface
  - Python
